---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const MCP = 'claude mcp add studio -- "/Applications/Life2Film Studio.app/Contents/MacOS/studio-cli" mcp';
const SKILL = `mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills/life2film-studio
cp "/Applications/Life2Film Studio.app/Contents/Resources/skill/life2film-studio/SKILL.md" \\
   ~/.claude/skills/life2film-studio/`;
const JSON_CFG = `{
  "mcpServers": {
    "studio": {
      "command": "/Applications/Life2Film Studio.app/Contents/MacOS/studio-cli",
      "args": ["mcp"]
    }
  }
}`;
const tools = [
  ['timeline_read', 'The film: its outline, a stretch, the words said, what is at a moment, the files behind it, loudness, notes'],
  ['timeline_frame', 'One frame at a moment, as the window shows it'],
  ['timeline_sheet', 'A sheet of frames over a stretch, or the inside of one source file'],
  ['timeline_propose', 'Edits checked against the film and kept beside it until you decide'],
  ['timeline_accept', 'Apply a proposal, after your yes'],
  ['timeline_reject', 'Take a proposal back, whole or its last idea'],
  ['timeline_undo', 'Put the film back as it was before the last accepted proposal'],
  ['timeline_note', 'Pin a note to a moment or reply to one'],
  ['timeline_new', 'Begin a film, or cut a reel out of another one'],
  ['timeline_mix', 'Render the sound with its plugin chains and measure it'],
  ['mix_plugins', 'The effects that exist, their knobs and ready-made chains'],
  ['timeline_film', 'Export: the whole film or a stretch, by preset'],
];
---
<Base lang="en" title="Connect an agent to Life2Film Studio" description="Life2Film Studio carries an MCP server with twelve tools. Register it with Claude Code or any MCP host, add the skill, and let an agent review a cut and propose edits you accept." basePath="/studio/agent" locales={['en']}>
  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="wrap prose">
        <p class="muted"><a href="/studio/">Studio</a> / Agent guide</p>
        <h1>Connect an agent</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Studio holds no model and no key. Inside the app is a small program that speaks MCP over stdio:
          no port, nothing listening, started by the agent host you already use. The agent reads the film,
          looks at frames and proposes edits. Only you accept them.
        </p>

        <h2>1. Register the door</h2>
        <p>With Claude Code:</p>
        <pre><code>{MCP}</code></pre>
        <p>With any other MCP host, the same program with the one argument <code>mcp</code>:</p>
        <pre><code>{JSON_CFG}</code></pre>

        <h2>2. Add the skill</h2>
        <p>
          The skill tells the agent how to work here: the order to read a film in, how pieces are named,
          what to check before asking you. It ships inside the app:
        </p>
        <pre><code>{SKILL}</code></pre>
        <p>
          Or read it here: <a href="/studio/skill/SKILL.md">SKILL.md</a>. The server also hands every agent
          its instructions on connect, plus three guides as MCP resources
          (<code>studio://guide/director</code>, <code>review</code>, <code>edits</code>), so a host without
          skills still gets the method.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Ask</h2>
        <p>Open the film in Studio so you can watch what happens, then ask in plain words. A few that work:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>“Review the cut at ~/Movies/trip/trip.otio. Propose nothing yet.”</li>
          <li>“At 14:36 the caption is wrong, it should say Seattle.”</li>
          <li>“Chapter 3 drags. Propose a version two minutes shorter.”</li>
          <li>“Bring the mix to -16 LUFS and tell me what you changed.”</li>
          <li>“Make a 60-second vertical from the Golden Gate chapter.”</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Two prompts come with the server and appear in the host’s prompt list:
          <code>direct_film</code> and <code>review_cut</code>.
        </p>

        <h2>What you see in the window</h2>
        <p>
          While an agent is connected, the window says so and marks the stretch it last looked at. A
          proposal is drawn on the timeline in amber: what goes is hatched, what comes in stands over the
          frame it would cover. Play shows the film as proposed before anything is written. Accept takes
          it, Drop Last removes the newest idea, and one undo puts the film back.
        </p>
        <p>
          Notes are the other half. Pin a note to a moment, or paste a list of timecodes from a viewing;
          each stays on its frame through edits, and the agent reads them as its brief the next time it
          opens the film.
        </p>

        <h2>The twelve tools</h2>
        <div class="tools">
          {tools.map(([n, d]) => (<div class="row"><code>{n}</code><span>{d}</span></div>))}
        </div>

        <h2>What the agent cannot do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Write the film without your accept.</li>
          <li>Look into files the film does not use.</li>
          <li>Hear. It reads loudness, pauses and measured levels, and says where a judgment needs ears.</li>
          <li>Touch your footage. Originals are only read.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { padding-top: 56px; }
  h1 { font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 48px); margin: 10px 0 18px; }
  .lede { font-size: 18px; color: #cfcfd6; max-width: 66ch; }
  pre {
    background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px;
    padding: 16px 18px; overflow-x: auto; margin: 12px 0 18px;
  }
  pre code { background: none; padding: 0; font-size: 13.5px; color: var(--cyan); }
  .tools { display: grid; gap: 8px; margin: 14px 0 8px; }
  .row { display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(150px, 190px) 1fr; gap: 14px; align-items: baseline;
         padding: 10px 14px; background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 10px; }
  .row span { color: #cfcfd6; font-size: 15px; }
  @media (max-width: 560px) { .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; } }
</style>
